<section class="section reveal">
  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#0984e3;margin-top:0.5rem">Workers (processes)</h3>
  <p>Parallelism across CPU cores. Multiple workers run simultaneously on different cores. The OS decides which worker handles each incoming connection. This is your horizontal scale — more workers means more CPU cores working.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#00b894;margin-top:1.5rem">Event loop (per worker)</h3>
  <p>Concurrency for I/O-bound work within each worker. One event loop handles many requests by interleaving them at <code>await</code> points. While one coroutine waits for a database response, another runs. This is why a single worker can handle hundreds of concurrent requests.</p>

  <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#6c5ce7;margin-top:1.5rem">Thread pool (per worker)</h3>
  <p>Handles sync/blocking code without freezing the event loop. When a <code>def</code> endpoint runs, FastAPI offloads it to a thread pool so the event loop stays free. The thread pool is not for parallelism — it's for isolation.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">Put it together:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">For I/O-bound work (99% of web APIs):</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">Event loop</span> handles concurrency within each worker.
  <span class="code-keyword">Workers</span>     handle parallelism across cores.
  <span class="code-string">Result:</span>     high throughput, low memory, all cores used.

<span class="code-comment">For CPU-bound work:</span>
  <span class="code-keyword">Workers</span>     handle parallelism.
  <span class="code-keyword">Event loop</span> gives no benefit (no I/O to interleave).
  <span class="code-string">Result:</span>     as many workers as cores, each pegging one core.</pre>
  </div>

  <p>This is why FastAPI scales well with reasonable hardware. It's not magic — it's three layers of well-understood concurrency working together.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 08: RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#dceeff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0a3d62">The GIL ensures only one thread runs Python at a time.</strong> Multiple threads in one process cannot achieve CPU parallelism in CPython.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">The fix is processes</strong> — each process has its own GIL and event loop. <code>uvicorn main:app --workers 4</code> creates four independent OS processes.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Each worker is isolated</strong> — its own memory, its own FastAPI app, its own state. Workers don't share anything. Global variables and in-process caches are per-worker.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fef5e7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Processes use more memory than threads</strong> — each worker loads a full copy of your app. That's the cost of true parallelism.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">The worker formula is 2 &times; CPU_cores + 1</strong> — enough to keep all cores busy, plus spares for workers momentarily blocked on I/O.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#ffeef7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e84393">
      <strong style="color:#e84393">Uvicorn <code>--workers</code> is sufficient for most setups.</strong> Gunicorn adds graceful restarts, crash recovery, and mature signal handling for high-traffic production.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e0fafa;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00cec9">
      <strong style="color:#00cec9">Three concurrency layers stack together:</strong> workers (parallelism across cores) &#8594; event loop (concurrency for I/O within each worker) &#8594; thread pool (isolation for sync code).
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep5">
  <span class="section-num part5-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <!-- Q1: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Worker 1 caches an API result in a Python dictionary. Worker 2 receives the next request for the same data. Does Worker 2 see Worker 1's cache?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Yes — all workers share the same memory</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Yes — Gunicorn syncs state between workers</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>No — each worker is a separate OS process with isolated memory</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>It depends on the caching library used</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) No — each worker is a separate OS process with isolated memory</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">When Gunicorn forks workers, each gets its own copy of everything. Global variables, in-memory caches, and module-level state are all per-process. If you need shared state, use an external store like Redis.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Put these steps in the correct order for Gunicorn startup:</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Workers start → Master binds port → Master forks → Requests arrive</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Master forks → Master binds port → Workers init → Requests arrive</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Master binds port → Master forks → Workers init app → Workers handle requests</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Master binds port → Workers init → Master forks → Requests arrive</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Master binds port → Master forks → Workers init app → Workers handle requests</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">This describes Gunicorn's pre-fork model specifically: the master owns the socket and passes it to forked workers via fork(), so workers inherit the file descriptor. Uvicorn's <code>--workers</code> flag uses spawn instead of fork, so each worker starts fresh and opens its own connection to the socket rather than inheriting it. Each worker still loads its own copy of the FastAPI application either way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3: True or False -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Adding more workers always improves performance linearly — double the workers, double the throughput.</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>True</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>False</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) False</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Returns diminish quickly. More workers than CPU cores means context-switching overhead. For CPU-bound work, the GIL limits each process to one thread of Python execution anyway. The recommended formula is <code>2 × CPU_cores + 1</code> — beyond that, you're often hurting performance rather than helping it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4: Multiple Choice -->
  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 04</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Your FastAPI app stores login sessions in a Python dictionary. You run 4 workers. A user logs in and their request is handled by Worker 1. Their next request hits Worker 3. Are they still logged in?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Yes — Gunicorn syncs session state between workers</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Yes — Python dictionaries are shared across processes</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>No — each worker has isolated memory, Worker 3 doesn't see Worker 1's dict</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>It depends on the session middleware configuration</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) No — each worker has isolated memory</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Worker 3 has its own separate Python dictionary that knows nothing about Worker 1's session. This is the classic "shared state" bug in multi-worker deployments. The fix: use an external session store (Redis, database) that all workers can access, or use sticky sessions at the load balancer level.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

